<!--
  FastDDS profile — SHM transport + DataSharing AUTOMATIC (the `zc`
  lane: FastDDS's TRUE zero-copy path). Audit fix C1.

  This is the rmw_fastrtps README's own zero-copy recipe, verbatim in
  shape (https://github.com/ros2/rmw_fastrtps — "Full support for
  Zero Copy" section): publisher/subscriber default profiles carrying
  <data_sharing><kind>AUTOMATIC</kind></data_sharing>, loaded with
  RMW_FASTRTPS_USE_QOS_FROM_XML=1 in the environment (run_bench.sh
  exports it for shm mode `zc` only). Because it is the vendor's own
  recommended configuration, this lane cannot be called tuning.

  Why a separate lane exists (and why the plain `shm` lane does NOT
  get DataSharing): rmw_fastrtps calls writer_qos.data_sharing().off()
  and reader_qos.data_sharing().off() inside
  `if (!participant_info->leave_middleware_default_qos)` on BOTH the
  jazzy and lyrical/rolling branches (publisher.cpp /
  subscription.cpp); leave_middleware_default_qos flips only with
  RMW_FASTRTPS_USE_QOS_FROM_XML=1. So out-of-box ROS 2 runs Fast DDS
  with its zero-copy mechanism forced off — the `shm` lane measures
  that stock-defaults behavior (labeled as such), and THIS lane
  measures Fast DDS at its vendor-recommended best.

  DataSharing vs the SHM transport (copy-count story): on the `shm`
  lane a sample rides loaned write -> SHM segment copy -> reader
  history copy -> loaned take; with DataSharing the writer history IS
  the shared segment — the documented constraints (bounded plain
  non-keyed type, PREALLOCATED* memory policy, reader depth <= writer
  depth) are all satisfied by the bench's fixed-size Pod<N> types
  under both QoS lanes (be1 = KEEP_LAST(1), rel10 = KEEP_LAST(10)).
  Reliability is NOT in Fast DDS's data-sharing constraint list.

  The SHM transport descriptor below is kept identical to
  fastdds_shm.xml (64 MiB segment) so the only delta between the two
  lanes is DataSharing itself: discovery still needs the builtin
  UDP transports, and any endpoint that fails the data-sharing
  eligibility gate falls back to the same tuned SHM transport the
  stock lane uses. There is NO per-size 16 MB segment switch on this
  lane — DataSharing bypasses the transport segment, fragmentation,
  and the >=1MB async FlowController entirely, which is also why this
  lane is the first thing to try where the 16 MB SHM-transport cell
  has never passed.

  NOTE RMW_FASTRTPS_USE_QOS_FROM_XML=1 also hands publish-mode and
  history-memory-policy control to the XML/Fast DDS defaults (the rmw
  stops forcing ASYNCHRONOUS publish mode + PREALLOCATED_WITH_REALLOC)
  — that is part of the vendor recipe, not a side effect we suppress.
  ROS-level QoS (reliability/durability/depth from bench_qos()) still
  applies to the DDS entities.

  Distro reality (verify on your machine; the verify_shm.sh probe fails LOUDLY
  either way): jazzy = Fast DDS 2.14.x, lyrical = 3.6.2 — data_sharing
  is a valid DataWriter/DataReader QoS element in both XSDs. Humble's
  apt Fast-DDS 2.6.x SEGFAULTED parsing these elements in the May-2026
  campaign (rc=139 on a one-shot `ros2 topic pub`) — a humble zc cell
  is expected to fail its verify probe loudly, keeping humble
  stock-defaults-only as a measured, documented limitation.

  Engagement evidence: DataSharing writer segments appear as
  /dev/shm/fast_datasharing_* while a zc cell runs; run_bench.sh's
  clean_shm removes stale ones between sizes (the `fastdds shm clean`
  convention).
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>shm_transport</transport_id>
        <type>SHM</type>
        <segment_size>67108864</segment_size>
        <port_queue_capacity>1024</port_queue_capacity>
        <healthy_check_timeout_ms>1000</healthy_check_timeout_ms>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>
    <participant profile_name="participant_default" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>shm_transport</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <!-- useBuiltinTransports=true is REQUIRED on Fast DDS 2.14+/3.x:
             PDP discovery always uses UDP/TCP even when SHM/DataSharing
             is enabled (Fast DDS SHM transport docs). Disabling builtin
             transports kills participant matching. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>true</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
    <publisher profile_name="datawriter_default" is_default_profile="true">
      <qos>
        <data_sharing>
          <kind>AUTOMATIC</kind>
        </data_sharing>
      </qos>
    </publisher>
    <subscriber profile_name="datareader_default" is_default_profile="true">
      <qos>
        <data_sharing>
          <kind>AUTOMATIC</kind>
        </data_sharing>
      </qos>
    </subscriber>
  </profiles>
</dds>
